<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Security</span><h1>Security</h1><p>PocketCat is designed with practical application controls for account protection, administrator separation, credential handling and safer defaults.</p></section><section class="section"><div class="section-inner">
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">01</span><h2>Authentication</h2><p>Passwords are stored using PHP password hashing rather than plaintext storage. Sessions use HTTP-only cookies and are regenerated on login to reduce session fixation risk.</p><p>Disabled accounts cannot authenticate through the normal login flow, while administrators can manage account status from the user control panel.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">02</span><h2>CSRF protection</h2><p>State-changing forms include a CSRF token and verify it on the server. This applies to account changes, content creation, administrator settings and other sensitive operations.</p><p>The public AI assistant also obtains its request token from the server-rendered page so the browser cannot submit arbitrary state-changing requests without the expected session context.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">03</span><h2>Secret storage</h2><p>Bedrock and SMTP secrets are kept server-side. New sensitive values are encrypted using an application secret and are not rendered back into the configuration form after saving.</p><p>Administrators should still use least-privilege credentials and rotate any credential that may have been exposed. The safest place for a secret is never a note, task description, public page or support message.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">04</span><h2>Web-server protection</h2><p>The package denies direct web access to database, include and storage directories and blocks common configuration files. The installation lock prevents the first-run wizard from becoming a public reconfiguration page.</p><p>Hosting configuration remains part of the security boundary. HTTPS, PHP updates, MySQL permissions and hosting account security should be maintained alongside the application.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">05</span><h2>Amazon Bedrock and AI safety</h2><p>Bedrock requests are initiated by server-side application code after the user is authenticated and usage controls have been checked. API keys and AWS signing credentials are not intended to be exposed in browser JavaScript or public HTML; saved secrets are encrypted at rest by the application.</p><p>AI output can be wrong or incomplete and must be reviewed before use in consequential decisions. Administrators should use least-privilege AWS permissions, select an appropriate AWS region and model, and consider the privacy implications of sending prompts to the configured provider.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">06</span><h2>Security reports and contact</h2><p>Report suspected security issues to <a href="mailto:support@pocketcat.online">support@pocketcat.online</a> with a concise description, affected page and safe reproduction details.</p><p>Do not include passwords, session cookies, API keys, private customer data or exploit payloads that could affect other users. Please allow the team reasonable time to assess a report before publicly disclosing details.</p></article>
</div></section><section class="cta"><span class="eyebrow" style="color:#9db8ff">POCKETCAT</span><h2>Explore the workspace.</h2><p>See the product, create an account or contact the team when you are ready.</p><a class="btn" href="/signup/">Get Started</a></section><?php public_footer(); ?>